@@ -8,6 +8,6 @@ Read the file at $ARGUMENTS and verify it follows these conventions:
 3. **Minimal comments**: Prefer descriptive function, variable and type names over prose — code should be self-documenting. TSDoc-style comments documenting parameters, return values and non-obvious contracts are acceptable. Flag comments that narrate a change that was made ("renamed from…", "now also handles…", "previously used X") — comments must describe the code as it currently stands; git history is where changes are examined. Also flag comments that merely restate what the code plainly says.
 4. **Server logging**: Backend should log whenever an decision is made, new data is retrieved. We want to be able to troubleshoot effectively.
 5. **Forms via @lib/ds only**: Forms must be built with the form primitives exported from `@lib/ds` (StandardForm, FormTextInput, FormPasswordInput, FormSubmitButton, FormGeneralErrors, useForm). Flag any direct import from `@mantine/form` outside of `libs/ds` — it should go through `@lib/ds` so the form library can be swapped later.
-6. **Admin backend isolation**: Admin backend code may live ONLY in one of three dedicated folders — `libs/admin-api` (`@lib/admin-api`), `libs/admin-contract` (`@lib/admin-contract`), or the `apps/admin-backend` app. The ONE exception: the admin DB table schemas (`app_admin`, `app_admin_session`) live in `@lib/db-models` alongside the other table definitions (the database layer is shared). Admin code must never live in other shared/regular app libraries (`@lib/identity`, `@lib/contract`, `@lib/server-commons`) or inline in a non-admin app (e.g. `apps/backend`). The admin section is intentionally self-contained so it can be extracted into its own app. Flag any admin-specific code — admin auth, admin sessions, admin routes/middleware/handlers — added outside those folders (admin DB tables in `@lib/db-models` are allowed), and flag any regular app library (other than `@lib/db-models` exposing the tables) importing from `@lib/admin-*`. A non-admin worker that merely hosts the admin app may only wire it in via a single `app.route("/", adminApp)` from `@lib/admin-api`.
+6. **Admin backend isolation**: Admin backend code may live ONLY in one of three dedicated folders — `libs/admin-api` (`@lib/admin-api`), `libs/admin-contract` (`@lib/admin-contract`), or the `apps/admin-backend` app. The ONE exception: the admin DB table schemas (`app_admin`, `app_admin_session`) live in `@lib/db-models` alongside the other table definitions (the database layer is shared). Admin code must never live in other shared/regular app libraries (`@lib/identity`, `@lib/contract`, `@lib/server-commons`) or inline in a non-admin app (e.g. `apps/backend`). The admin section is self-contained and runs in its own Worker. Flag any admin-specific code — admin auth, admin sessions, admin routes/middleware/handlers — added outside those folders (admin DB tables in `@lib/db-models` are allowed), and flag any regular app library (other than `@lib/db-models` exposing the tables) importing from `@lib/admin-*`. Only `apps/admin-backend` mounts `adminApp`; flag any other app (above all `apps/backend`) importing `@lib/admin-*`.
 7. **Prefer small, mature libraries over hand-rolled low-level code**: For well-solved low-level functionality (date math, UUID, deep clone/equal, debounce/throttle, query-string parsing, result/option types, slugify, etc.), use a mature, industry-standard library instead of writing your own — PROVIDED its footprint is small (a few kB, ~10–20 kB gzipped max; tree-shakeable preferred). Before adding a dependency, use the `check-library-to-install` subagent (auto-delegated when a dependency decision comes up) to vet candidates (fit, docs, Snyk maturity/security, bundle size) and pick per its recommendation. Flag a new hand-rolled implementation of a well-solved low-level problem where a small mature library exists (e.g. re-implementing date arithmetic instead of a dedicated date library, a custom result-type union instead of a maintained one), and flag pulling in a heavy dependency (>~20 kB gzipped, or one with poor Snyk health/maintenance) for a small need. Trivial one-liners (a `padStart` time formatter, a lexicographic string compare) may stay inline — this is about non-trivial functionality, not banning all local helpers.
    Fix any violations found. Do not re-run this skill after fixing — the post-edit hook will fire again but you should ignore it for files you just fixed via this skill. If no violations, confirm the file follows conventions.
